# Git and PR conventions

## Commits

Use Conventional Commits:

- `feat:` — new user-facing capability
- `fix:` — bug fix
- `chore:` — tooling, deps, busywork
- `docs:` — documentation / ADRs
- `refactor:` — no behavior change intended
- `test:` — tests only
- `ci:` — workflows

## Branching

- **Work directly on `qa`.** Do not create feature branches for routine work.
- **Do not commit directly to `main`.** Promote `qa` → `main` via the `Promote QA to production` workflow (fast-forward only) after QA looks good.
- Keep history readable; prefer linear fast-forward promotion from `qa`.

## Every promotion / PR must include

1. **What** changed
2. **Why** it changed
3. **How** it was tested (commands, CI, manual checks)
4. **Docs** — which `docs/architecture/` pages changed (or N/A with one-line why)

Contract / OpenAPI changes must update `packages/shared-types` and web consumers in the **same change**.

## Architecture-relevant changes

When the change touches boundaries, endpoints, data model, ingest, admin, reader routes, OpenAPI/shared-types, or hosting/env:

- [ ] Atlas page(s) updated (or N/A with one-line why)
- [ ] Mermaid still accurate
- [ ] “Last verified” bumped on edited pages
- [ ] Hub index updated if a new page was added
